d <- H1("2. Methods")
d <- H2("2.1 Samples")
d <- P("Carriers of 16p11.2 proximal (BP4-BP5) deletions and duplications and 22q11.2 A-D deletions and duplications were taken from the clinical growth genomatrix (cohorts G2MH, Cardiff, SVIP, Searchlight; G2MH rows masked as Cardiff duplicates removed), ages 2-65, using the latest visit at which the outcome and BMI were both available. ASD status came from the G2MH subject table (Jon/Claude/G2MH_CNV_growth/subjects.csv) and the SVIP diagnosis summary. Unaffected controls were non-carrier SSC designated siblings and parents (sibling height, weight and head circumference from the SSC sibling table; SSC, PMID 20955926) and SVIP non-carrier family members without ASD; no SSC probands and no UK Biobank data are in the current figures. SSC probands with ASD and no CNV served as a separate ASD comparison group. Social-communication and cognitive measures were available for G2MH carriers (SRS-2 total, SCQ lifetime, RBS-R, FSIQ, Penn CNB; PMIDs 10832778, 19900482, 25180981) and for SSC relatives and probands (SRS, SCQ).")
d <- H2("2.2 Growth scoring, ages and quality control")
d <- P("Height was scored against the WHO child growth standards and the WHO 2007 reference (PMIDs 16817681, 18026621), head circumference against the UK 1990 reference with adults scored at 18 years (PMID 9496720), child BMI against WHO, and adult BMI as a centile against the pooled non-carrier adults of the clinical cohorts mapped to a Z score (00_scoring.R; childsds R package).")
d <- P("Two data issues were found and corrected during this work. (i) The SSC v15 age columns are ages at a single snapshot date (September 2012), about three years older than the study visit. Sibling and parent visit age was reconstructed as the proband's age at evaluation plus the birth-month difference, which reproduces SSC's own sibling height Z (r = 0.96); implemented in 21_pheno_bmi_gam.R and 01_build.R and documented in README_ages_and_line_endings.md in the SSC folder. (ii) Implausible raw values (heights recorded in metres, heights of 366-640 cm, adult BMI of 60-181, a BMI of 9.2, children with height or BMI Z beyond 5) are screened by 00_qc_growth.R before scoring: metre-coded heights are multiplied by 100; heights outside 60-230 cm (with their BMI), BMI outside 10-70, |height Z| > 5, child |BMI Z| > 5, and head circumference outside 30-70 cm or |Z| > 5 are set to missing. The 106 flagged visit rows (95 individuals) are listed in implausible_height_weight.csv/.md; a correction sheet (implausible_height_weight_corrections.csv) is applied automatically by the QC once filled in. Pre-QC outputs are kept in preQC_20261024/.")
d <- H2("2.3 Statistical models (cross-sectional; main figure, Tables S1-S2)")
d <- P("All models are generalised additive models in mgcv (REML; bam with fREML for the carrier-versus-control fits). BMI enters as a continuous Z score; the 70th/30th centile split in the figures is for display only. Within a group: BMI main effect, y ~ BMI + s(age) [+ locus], giving the BMI slope in SD of outcome per SD of BMI; and age x BMI, y ~ s(age) + s(BMI) + ti(age, BMI) [+ locus]. Carriers versus a reference group: CNV x BMI, the age-adjusted difference in BMI slope, y ~ [locus +] CNV + s(age, by = CNV) + s(age, by = BMI) + CNV:BMI; and CNV x age x BMI, y ~ [locus +] CNV + s(age) + s(BMI) + s(age, by = CNV) + ti(age, BMI) + ti(age, BMI, by = CNV), reported as the p-value of the carrier-specific tensor product. Pooled groups (overgrowth = 16p11.2 del + 22q11.2 dup; undergrowth = 16p11.2 dup + 22q11.2 del; all) include locus as a covariate. Heterogeneity of the BMI slope across the four CNV groups was tested within carriers as the Wald test of the locus x BMI interaction in y ~ s(age, by = locus) + locus + BMI + locus:BMI. For SRS and SCQ the trait is plotted against BMI after removing the within-group age smooth, because the BMI effects on these traits do not depend on age.")
d <- H2("2.4 Longitudinal (prospective) models (Supplementary figure, Table S3)")
d <- P("In carriers under 18 years, the change in height Z or head-circumference Z from one visit to the next was regressed on BMI Z at the first visit, adjusting for Z at the visit, interval length, sex and (pooled) locus and cohort, with a child random intercept. A mixed GAM with s(age, by = BMI) gives the age-varying effect (age-varying p: BMI effect differs from zero at any age); the same model with a constant BMI term gives the all-ages coefficient; fixed age windows (height: < 8, 8-13, 13-18 y with lmer; head circumference: < 2, 2-5, 5-18 y) give window coefficients. Height used cleaned longitudinal visits of SVIP, Searchlight and Cardiff (intervals >= 0.5 y); head circumference used the full SVIP v10 series plus Cardiff, G2MH and SSC carrier visits (01_build.R, 05_age_varying.R, 07_hc_trajectories.R, 11_supp_prospective.R, 29_supp_table_S3_prospective.R).")
d <- H2("2.5 Medication")
d <- P("Current medication from the G2MH medications release was classed by drug name (ATC codes were empty) into antipsychotics, stimulant/ADHD medication, ADHD alpha-agonists, antidepressants, antiepileptics/mood stabilisers and anxiolytics; weight-gain-associated (antipsychotics, valproate, lithium, mirtazapine, gabapentinoids) and weight-loss-associated (stimulants, atomoxetine, topiramate, metformin, GLP-1 agonists) groupings were also formed. Status was known for 87-92% of carriers. BMI slopes were refit unadjusted, with class indicators, in carriers on no psychotropic, and with classes plus ASD (27_medication_sensitivity.R).")
d <- H2("2.6 Polygenic scores")
d <- P("Weights were SBayesRC posterior effects (PMID 38689000; ukbEUR_HM3 LD reference, baseline 2.2 annotations) computed by Anjali Srinivasan from published summary statistics: adult BMI (Yengo 2018, PMID 30124842), childhood obesity (Bradfield 2019, trans-ancestral, PMID 31504550), childhood BMI (Vogelezang 2020, PMID 33045005), height (Yengo 2022, PMID 36224396), and autism from the April 2026 ASD GWAS (iPSYCH, PGC, SPARK, SSC, MoBa; unpublished, J. Grove; the 2019 release is PMID 30804558). G2MH WGS carriers were scored by Anjali with plink2 (PMID 25722852) and, for autism, scored in this work with the full 2026 weights because G2MH is not a GWAS cohort. SSC controls were scored in this work on the SSC genotype set with the same obesity, childhood BMI, adult BMI and height weights, and for autism with the leave-SSC-out weights, because SSC is in the GWAS (rule in jsebat/sumstats/README.md). Scores were residualised on 10 ancestry principal components within cohort (G2MH: projected PCs; SSC: PCs from the combined ADSI table) and z-scored within cohort. Carriers from SVIP, Searchlight and Cardiff have no scores in this pipeline. The three obesity/BMI scores are moderately correlated (childhood obesity vs childhood BMI r = 0.62; each vs adult BMI r = 0.33); height and autism scores are uncorrelated with them.")
d <- H2("2.7 Sex and polygenic-score sensitivity analyses (Tables S4-S6)")
d <- P("Three complementary analyses (30a_build_ssc_pgs.R, 30_sex_pgs_sensitivity.R). Table S5, the confounding check: for each trait and group, the carrier BMI slope and the CNV x BMI shift versus unaffected controls were refit on the same individuals (G2MH carriers and SSC controls with all four scores) unadjusted; adding sex and sex x BMI; adding each PGS with its BMI interaction; adding all four PGS; and all four plus sex. Table S4, strata: the main-figure tests repeated within sex and within below/above-median halves of each PGS, with carriers and controls stratified alike. Table S6, power: for each modifier (sex; each PGS as a continuous z-score) the interaction term BMI x modifier was fitted within carriers (y ~ s(age) + BMI * modifier [+ locus]) and the power to detect an interaction as large as the carriers' own BMI effect was computed from the term's standard error (normal approximation), with the smallest interaction detectable at 80% power (2.8 x SE). Interactions are reported only where that power is at least 80%.")
d <- H2("2.8 Autism diagnosis within CNV group (supplementary figure fig_supp_asd_by_cnv)")
d <- P("32_supp_asd_by_cnv.R. Within each CNV group (per locus; pooled overgrowth = 16p11.2 deletion + 22q11.2 duplication; pooled undergrowth = 16p11.2 duplication + 22q11.2 deletion) height Z, head-circumference Z and BMI Z (same scoring and QC as the main figure; latest visit with the outcome and BMI, ages 2-65) were compared between carriers with and without an autism diagnosis (G2MH subject table, SVIP diagnosis summary) with a GAM, y ~ ASD + s(age, k = 8) + sex, plus cohort and, in pooled groups, locus. The non-carrier comparison is SSC probands (ASD) versus SSC designated siblings. Because the ASD carriers are mostly children and non-ASD carriers include adult relatives, the age smooth is essential; 20_asd_age_confound.R shows the head-circumference effect holds within children. Sensitivity: in the individuals with all four polygenic scores (G2MH carriers; SSC non-carriers; scores as in 2.6) each model was refitted with the same covariates, then adding the childhood obesity, adult BMI, height and autism PGS one at a time and all four together, on the same individuals (supp_asd_by_cnv_sensitivity.csv; sheet 'S8 ASD by CNV PGS adj'). Sex is a covariate in every model, so sex confounding is addressed by design rather than by stratification.")
d <- H2("2.9 Software and run order")
d <- P("R 4.6.1 with data.table, mgcv (Wood 2011, J R Stat Soc B 73:3-36), lme4 and lmerTest, childsds, ggplot2, patchwork, openxlsx, officer; plink2 (PMID 25722852) and Slurm on Expanse. Run order after any data change: 01 -> 05 -> 07 -> 11 -> 29, then 23 -> 25 -> 26 -> 27 -> 28 -> 30 -> 32.")

d <- H1("3. Results")
d <- H2("3.1 Main figure (fig_main_v3): height, head circumference, SRS and SCQ versus unaffected relatives")
d <- B("Head circumference. 16p11.2 deletion is the only locus whose head-circumference-BMI coupling exceeds that of unaffected relatives (CNV x BMI +0.13 SD per SD, p = 0.021; CNV x age x BMI p = 0.020; against SSC ASD probands +0.17, p = 0.002). Head circumference rises with BMI in every group including controls (+0.36 SD per SD), so the 16p11.2 deletion effect is an excess over a general relationship; it is present in both sexes and in carriers without ASD.")
d <- B("Height. No locus shows a constant CNV x BMI shift versus controls after QC (16p11.2 del p = 0.99, 16p11.2 dup 0.24, 22q11.2 del 0.48, 22q11.2 dup 0.99). The CNV x age x BMI terms are nominally significant for 16p11.2 dup and 22q11.2 dup but proved unstable to the removal of a few extreme values and are not emphasised (section 3.6).")
d <- B("SRS and SCQ. Scores rise with BMI in carriers but not in unaffected relatives: all CNVs versus controls SRS +0.21 SD per SD (p = 1.6e-6), SCQ +0.34 (p = 4.8e-5); 22q11.2 deletion SRS +0.23 (p = 8.4e-6), SCQ +0.27 (p = 0.001); 16p11.2 duplication SRS +0.50 (p = 3.8e-5, n = 34). SSC probands with ASD and no CNV show no BMI slope (SRS +0.03, SCQ +0.03), so the association is specific to CNV carriers rather than to ASD. There is no heterogeneity of the slope across the four CNV groups (SRS p = 0.61, SCQ p = 0.96). Restricting to carriers without an ASD diagnosis attenuates the SRS slope but not the SCQ slope; SCQ (and SRS in some cohorts) contributed to ASD assignment, so that restriction selects on the outcome.")
d <- B("Penn CNB (supplementary figures). No robust BMI effects on accuracy or speed for any subtest or total. The 16p11.2 duplication accuracy slope (-0.14, p = 0.07) and the across-CNV heterogeneity for the accuracy total (p = 0.11) lost significance when one implausible BMI value was removed by QC; the result is reported as suggestive only.")
d <- H2("3.2 Longitudinal analysis (fig_supp_prospective, Table S3)")
d <- P("Higher BMI at a visit precedes faster height growth in 16p11.2 deletion carriers under 8 years (+0.23 SD of height Z per SD of BMI Z to the next visit, t = 3.9, p = 1.6e-4; age-varying p = 0.006; pooled carriers age-varying p = 0.022, < 8 y window +0.13, t = 2.9). For head circumference the effect is positive at all ages (all-ages coefficient, 16p11.2 deletion +0.08, t = 2.1, p = 0.038; pooled +0.10, t = 3.4, p = 7e-4; age-varying p = 7.5e-4). Height results were unchanged by QC; head circumference lost five SVIP intervals and strengthened slightly.")
d <- H2("3.3 Medication")
d <- P("Psychotropic use was common (29-33% of carriers; antipsychotics 5-7%) and strongly associated with the traits, but no medication class was associated with BMI, and the BMI slopes on SRS and SCQ were unchanged by adjustment for medication classes or by excluding medicated carriers (SRS all CNVs +0.22, +0.19, +0.23; SCQ +0.35, +0.37, +0.38). ASD diagnosis, not medication, is the only covariate that attenuates the SRS slope. Details: medication_sensitivity_results.md.")
d <- H2("3.4 Sex and polygenic background: confounding is excluded (Table S5)")
d <- P("Adjusting for sex, for any single PGS, for all four PGS (childhood obesity, adult BMI, height, autism), or for all four plus sex leaves the BMI effects essentially unchanged on the same individuals. CNV x BMI versus unaffected controls (estimate, SE, p):")
TB(data.frame(`Trait, group` = c("Head circumference, 16p11.2 del", "SRS, all CNVs", "SRS, 22q11.2 del", "SCQ, all CNVs", "SCQ, 22q11.2 del", "Height, all CNVs"), `n carriers / controls` = c("98 / 4,985", "349 / 4,988", "186 / 4,988", "268 / 1,030", "151 / 1,030", "550 / 5,010"),
  Unadjusted = c("+0.130 (0.076), p = 0.086", "+0.192 (0.048), p = 5.8e-5", "+0.293 (0.055), p = 1.3e-7", "+0.318 (0.091), p = 5.0e-4", "+0.350 (0.091), p = 1.2e-4", "+0.009 (0.039), p = 0.81"),
  `+ sex` = c("+0.139, p = 0.065", "+0.194, p = 5.0e-5", "+0.292, p = 1.4e-7", "+0.311, p = 6.6e-4", "+0.339, p = 1.9e-4", "+0.010, p = 0.80"),
  `+ all four PGS` = c("+0.142, p = 0.057", "+0.196, p = 4.1e-5", "+0.292, p = 1.4e-7", "+0.318, p = 5.6e-4", "+0.347, p = 1.4e-4", "+0.013, p = 0.70"),
  `+ all four PGS + sex` = c("+0.151, p = 0.041", "+0.198, p = 3.6e-5", "+0.292, p = 1.5e-7", "+0.312, p = 7.1e-4", "+0.337, p = 2.2e-4", "+0.014, p = 0.66"), check.names = FALSE))
d <- P("In this subset (G2MH carriers with scores and SSC controls) the 16p11.2 deletion head-circumference contrast is weaker than in the full sample (p = 0.086 versus 0.021) because the SVIP, Searchlight and Cardiff carriers are absent; adjustment moves it towards, not away from, significance. PGS main effects on SRS and SCQ are null. The stratified analysis (Table S4) shows the carrier-versus-control slopes in both sexes and in both halves of every PGS split.")
d <- H2("3.5 Interactions: power and findings (Table S6)")
d <- P("Of 206 modifier x BMI interaction tests, 47 had at least 80% power to detect an interaction as large as the carriers' own BMI effect: all PGS and sex for head circumference (pooled and per locus; interaction SE 0.04-0.13), all PGS for height in the pooled, 16p11.2 deletion and overgrowth groups (SE 0.035-0.09), and a few SRS rows (autism PGS pooled, 81%; adult BMI and height PGS in 22q11.2 deletion and undergrowth, 82-86%). Sex x BMI for height (17%), every SCQ row (43-71%), the remaining SRS rows (61-77%) and every CNB row (< 25%) were underpowered, and no interaction is reported for them.")
d <- P("Among the 47 well-powered tests, seven were nominally significant and none survives correction for 47 tests (p < 0.001): adult BMI PGS x BMI on height (all CNVs +0.088 per SD of PGS, p = 0.017; 16p11.2 del +0.13, p = 0.048; overgrowth +0.12, p = 0.019) and on head circumference (all CNVs +0.089, p = 0.031; overgrowth +0.13, p = 0.027); childhood obesity PGS and height PGS x BMI on head circumference in 22q11.2 deletion (-0.15, p = 0.024; -0.14, p = 0.028); and autism PGS x BMI on SRS (all CNVs -0.18, p = 0.019). The adult BMI PGS effect has a consistent direction (stronger BMI-growth coupling with higher polygenic BMI) across groups and traits and is the one candidate worth following up in a larger sample; the others are isolated. There is no credible evidence of sex- or PGS-dependent BMI effects, and the main-figure results are not explained by sex or polygenic background.")
d <- H2("3.6 Effect of the plausibility QC")
d <- P("SRS and SCQ results changed by at most 0.03 SD per SD. The 16p11.2 deletion head-circumference effect persisted. The 16p11.2 duplication CNB accuracy slope and the across-CNV heterogeneity lost significance. Several height CNV x age x BMI p-values moved by orders of magnitude in both directions when a handful of extreme heights were removed, so the three-way terms are reported but not emphasised (section 9 of unaffected_controls_report.md).")

d <- H2("3.7 Autism diagnosis within CNV group (fig_supp_asd_by_cnv; sheets S7-S8)")
d <- P("Head circumference is larger in carriers with than without autism in the overgrowth CNVs (pooled: Delta +0.45 SD, p = 0.022, n = 188 non-ASD / 96 ASD; 16p11.2 deletion +0.41, p = 0.074; 22q11.2 duplication +0.59, p = 0.16) but not in the undergrowth CNVs (pooled -0.05, p = 0.8); SSC probands also have slightly larger heads than their siblings (+0.14, p = 0.0014). Height does not differ by autism status in any group (all p > 0.07). BMI does not differ by autism status within any CNV group (per locus Delta +0.10 to +0.16, p 0.43-0.78; pooled overgrowth +0.08, p = 0.69; undergrowth +0.15, p = 0.36), whereas SSC probands are marginally heavier than their siblings (+0.11, p = 0.032). So the BMI-related effects in the main figure are not a by-product of the ASD carriers being heavier than non-ASD carriers.")
d <- P("Sensitivity (same individuals, with all four PGS): adding childhood obesity, adult BMI, height and autism PGS, singly or together, to the age- and sex-adjusted model leaves every ASD contrast essentially unchanged. Head circumference, overgrowth: +0.33 (p = 0.20) with age and sex, +0.36 (p = 0.16) with all four PGS (n = 101 / 68; the contrast is attenuated in this subset because SVIP, Searchlight and Cardiff carriers are unscored); 16p11.2 deletion +0.33 to +0.37. BMI: overgrowth +0.07 to +0.13; undergrowth +0.32 (p = 0.092) to +0.35 (p = 0.058); 16p11.2 deletion +0.14 to +0.19; non-carriers +0.13 (p = 0.014) to +0.10 (p = 0.037). Height: carrier contrasts unchanged; among SSC non-carriers the proband-minus-sibling height difference becomes more negative with height PGS in the model (-0.09, p = 0.056, to -0.14, p = 0.00034), i.e. probands are slightly shorter than predicted from their height PGS. Neither sex nor polygenic background explains the within-CNV ASD effects on head circumference or the absence of ASD effects on BMI.")
d <- H1("4. Figures and legends")
d <- P("Full legends are in figure_legends.md in the materials folder and beside each figure as *_legend.txt.")
d <- B("Main figure (fig_main_v3.png/.pdf; 25_main_figure_v3.R): columns Height, Head circumference, SRS, SCQ; rows unaffected controls, 16p11.2 del, 16p11.2 dup, 22q11.2 del, 22q11.2 dup. A-J: trajectories by BMI tail with CNV x BMI and CNV x age x BMI p-values versus controls. K-T: age-adjusted trait versus BMI Z with the slope and the p-value of its difference from controls; K and P also show SSC ASD probands. The legend includes the across-CNV heterogeneity test.")
d <- B("Supplementary prospective figure (fig_supp_prospective; 11_supp_prospective.R): age-varying prospective effect of BMI on subsequent height and head-circumference growth in carriers < 18 y; Table S3 gives effects, SE, t and p.")
d <- B("Supplementary SRS/SCQ figure (fig_cognition_bmi_slopes; 24_cognition_bmi_slopes.R): SRS and SCQ versus BMI with unaffected relatives and SSC ASD probands, plus a forest plot of slopes by group (all carriers, non-ASD carriers, ASD-adjusted).")
d <- B("Supplementary CNB figures (fig_supp_cnb_accuracy_total_bmi; fig_supp_cnb_accuracy_bmi; fig_supp_cnb_speed_bmi; 26_supp_cnb_bmi.R): accuracy total by CNV group with the heterogeneity test in the legend; all accuracy and speed subtests.")
d <- B("Supplementary ASD-by-CNV figure (fig_supp_asd_by_cnv; 32_supp_asd_by_cnv.R): mean +/- SE height, head circumference and BMI Z in carriers with versus without an autism diagnosis for each CNV group, the pooled overgrowth and undergrowth groups and SSC probands versus siblings, with the age- and sex-adjusted ASD effect and p-value printed; the legend reports the PGS-adjusted sensitivity analysis.")

d <- H1("5. Supplementary tables")
